{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Apex language fundamentals</h2>
<p>Apex is a strongly-typed, object-oriented language that runs on Salesforce servers. Its syntax is deliberately Java-like, so classes, methods, variables, and control flow will feel familiar. What makes it different is the environment: Apex is <strong>multitenant</strong>, meaning your code shares infrastructure with every other customer, so the platform enforces strict <strong>governor limits</strong> to keep any one tenant from monopolising resources. Writing good Apex is largely about respecting those limits.</p>
<p>Apex is case-insensitive and statements end with semicolons. Primitive types include <code>Integer</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Decimal</code>, <code>Double</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>Datetime</code>, and <code>Id</code>. sObjects represent records — <code>Account</code>, <code>Contact</code>, and your custom <code>__c</code> objects are all types you can declare and instantiate. Control flow uses the usual <code>if/else</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, and <code>switch on</code> statements.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">public class GreetingService {
    // A simple method returning a String
    public static String greet(String name) {
        if (String.isBlank(name)) {
            name = 'there';
        }
        return 'Hello, ' + name + '!';
    }
}

// Invoke it from anonymous Apex or a test
String message = GreetingService.greet('Ada');
System.debug(message); // Hello, Ada!</code></pre>
<p>Notice a few conventions. Methods and classes declare an access modifier (<code>public</code>, <code>private</code>, <code>global</code>). <code>static</code> methods belong to the class rather than an instance. <code>System.debug()</code> writes to the debug log, your primary tool for inspecting execution. Apex runs in a specific <strong>context</strong> — a trigger, a controller, a batch job, an anonymous script — and that context determines which limits apply.</p>
<p>The single most important habit to build now is thinking in <strong>bulk</strong>. Apex frequently runs against many records at once, so you'll write code that operates on collections rather than one record at a time. Keep that in mind as we cover collections next; almost every governor limit problem traces back to doing per-record work inside a loop instead of set-based work across a collection.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Collections: List, Set &amp; Map</h2>
<p>Collections are the backbone of bulk-safe Apex. Three types cover almost everything. A <strong>List</strong> is an ordered collection that allows duplicates and is indexed from zero — perfect for query results and DML. A <strong>Set</strong> is an unordered collection of unique values, ideal for de-duplicating Ids or checking membership quickly. A <strong>Map</strong> stores key-value pairs and gives you fast lookups by key, which is the secret to avoiding nested queries.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">// List: ordered, allows duplicates
List&lt;String&gt; names = new List&lt;String&gt;{ 'Ada', 'Grace', 'Ada' };
names.add('Linus');

// Set: unique values, great for Ids
Set&lt;Id&gt; accountIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();
for (Contact c : contacts) {
    accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
}

// Map: fast lookup by key
Map&lt;Id, Account&gt; accountById = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;(
    [SELECT Id, Name FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
);
for (Contact c : contacts) {
    Account parent = accountById.get(c.AccountId);
    System.debug(c.LastName + ' works at ' + parent.Name);
}</code></pre>
<p>The Map pattern above is worth internalising. Instead of querying an account inside a loop over contacts — which would multiply your SOQL usage and blow the query limit — you collect the parent Ids into a Set, run a single query, load the results into a <code>Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;</code>, and then look each parent up in memory. One query serves any number of contacts. This "query once, look up in a map" technique appears in almost every well-written trigger.</p>
<p>A few more essentials. The <code>Map&lt;Id, SObject&gt;</code> constructor that takes a query directly, as shown, keys records by their Id automatically. Sets and Map keys use value equality for primitives. Lists support useful methods like <code>size()</code>, <code>isEmpty()</code>, <code>sort()</code>, and <code>add()</code>. You can nest collections — a <code>Map&lt;Id, List&lt;Contact&gt;&gt;</code> groups children by parent, another extremely common shape.</p>
<p>Choosing the right collection is a design decision. Reach for a List when order matters or you'll do DML, a Set when you need uniqueness or membership tests, and a Map when you need to relate two sets of records efficiently. Get comfortable here and the SOQL, trigger, and bulkification lessons that follow will feel natural.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>DML &amp; the save order of execution</h2>
<p>DML (Data Manipulation Language) is how Apex changes records: <code>insert</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>upsert</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>undelete</code>, and <code>merge</code>. The golden rule mirrors SOQL — operate on collections, never inside a loop. You can pass a single sObject or, far better, a <code>List&lt;SObject&gt;</code> so one DML statement handles up to 10,000 records within a transaction's limits.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">List&lt;Account&gt; toUpdate = new List&lt;Account&gt;();
for (Account a : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Industry = 'Technology']) {
    a.Rating = 'Hot';
    toUpdate.add(a);
}
// One DML call for the whole list
update toUpdate;

// Partial success with Database methods
Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);
for (Database.SaveResult r : results) {
    if (!r.isSuccess()) {
        for (Database.Error err : r.getErrors()) {
            System.debug('Failed: ' + err.getMessage());
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>There are two flavours of DML. The keyword form (<code>update toUpdate;</code>) throws a <code>DmlException</code> and rolls back the whole operation if any record fails — all or nothing. The <code>Database</code> methods (<code>Database.update(list, false)</code>) let you allow partial success: valid records save, failures come back as results you can inspect and handle. Choose based on whether a single bad record should sink the batch.</p>
<p>The <strong>save order of execution</strong> is the sequence Salesforce follows every time a record is saved, and knowing it prevents baffling bugs. Roughly: the record loads, system validation runs, before-save flows fire, <strong>before triggers</strong> run, custom validation rules and duplicate rules run, the record saves to the database (not yet committed), <strong>after triggers</strong> run, assignment and auto-response rules run, workflow field updates fire (which can re-trigger before/after update triggers), then processes, escalation, roll-up summary recalculations, sharing evaluation, and finally the commit.</p>
<p>Two implications matter constantly. First, in a <strong>before trigger</strong> you can modify fields on the incoming records for free — no DML needed — because the save hasn't happened yet. Second, workflow field updates can cause triggers to run a second time, so idempotent, recursion-guarded trigger logic matters. Keep this order in mind and the trigger patterns in the next lesson will make complete sense.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Triggers &amp; the handler pattern</h2>
<p>A trigger is Apex that runs automatically when records are inserted, updated, deleted, or undeleted. Triggers fire in <strong>before</strong> or <strong>after</strong> contexts and expose context variables: <code>Trigger.new</code> (the new versions), <code>Trigger.old</code> (the previous versions), <code>Trigger.newMap</code> and <code>Trigger.oldMap</code> (Id-keyed maps), plus booleans like <code>Trigger.isInsert</code> and <code>Trigger.isBefore</code>. Because triggers always operate on batches of up to 200 records, they must be bulk-safe from the first line.</p>
<p>The widely accepted best practice is <strong>one trigger per object</strong> containing no business logic — it simply delegates to a handler class. This keeps logic testable, ordered, and free of the chaos that multiple competing triggers on the same object create.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">trigger AccountTrigger on Account (before insert, before update, after update) {
    AccountTriggerHandler handler = new AccountTriggerHandler();
    if (Trigger.isBefore &amp;&amp; Trigger.isInsert) {
        handler.onBeforeInsert(Trigger.new);
    } else if (Trigger.isAfter &amp;&amp; Trigger.isUpdate) {
        handler.onAfterUpdate(Trigger.new, Trigger.oldMap);
    }
}

public class AccountTriggerHandler {
    public void onBeforeInsert(List&lt;Account&gt; newAccounts) {
        for (Account a : newAccounts) {
            if (String.isBlank(a.Rating)) a.Rating = 'Warm';
        }
    }
    public void onAfterUpdate(List&lt;Account&gt; newAccounts, Map&lt;Id, Account&gt; oldMap) {
        List&lt;Task&gt; tasks = new List&lt;Task&gt;();
        for (Account a : newAccounts) {
            if (a.Rating == 'Hot' &amp;&amp; oldMap.get(a.Id).Rating != 'Hot') {
                tasks.add(new Task(WhatId = a.Id, Subject = 'Follow up: now Hot'));
            }
        }
        if (!tasks.isEmpty()) insert tasks;
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Several patterns are on display. The trigger routes each context to a specific handler method, so logic lives in a class you can unit-test directly. Field defaulting happens in the <strong>before</strong> context with no DML. Detecting a <em>change</em> compares <code>Trigger.new</code> against <code>oldMap</code> — here we only act when Rating became "Hot", not on every update. DML is done once on a collection after the loop, never inside it.</p>
<p>Mature teams add a little more structure: a trigger framework that guarantees ordering and provides recursion control so field updates that re-fire the trigger don't loop forever. But the essence is this handler pattern. Keep triggers thin, keep logic in handlers, and always assume 200 records. Next we'll stress-test these ideas against governor limits and true bulkification.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Bulkification &amp; governor limits</h2>
<p>Because Salesforce is multitenant, it caps the resources any transaction can consume through <strong>governor limits</strong>. The most commonly hit limits per synchronous transaction include 100 SOQL queries, 150 DML statements, 50,000 records retrieved by SOQL, and a total heap size of 6 MB. Exceed one and the platform throws an uncatchable <code>LimitException</code> and rolls the transaction back. <strong>Bulkification</strong> is the practice of writing code that stays comfortably under these limits regardless of how many records it processes.</p>
<p>Almost every limit problem comes from the same anti-pattern: doing per-record work inside a loop. The fix is always to move queries and DML outside loops and operate on collections instead.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">// ANTI-PATTERN: query and DML inside a loop -&gt; hits limits at scale
for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {
    Account a = [SELECT Id, AnnualRevenue FROM Account WHERE Id = :opp.AccountId];
    a.AnnualRevenue = (a.AnnualRevenue == null ? 0 : a.AnnualRevenue) + opp.Amount;
    update a; // 1 query + 1 DML per opportunity!
}

// BULKIFIED: one query, one DML, any number of records
Set&lt;Id&gt; accIds = new Set&lt;Id&gt;();
for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) accIds.add(opp.AccountId);

Map&lt;Id, Account&gt; accById = new Map&lt;Id, Account&gt;(
    [SELECT Id, AnnualRevenue FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accIds]
);
for (Opportunity opp : Trigger.new) {
    Account a = accById.get(opp.AccountId);
    a.AnnualRevenue = (a.AnnualRevenue == null ? 0 : a.AnnualRevenue) + opp.Amount;
}
update accById.values();</code></pre>
<p>The bulkified version collects parent Ids into a Set, queries once into a Map, accumulates changes in memory, and issues a single <code>update</code> on the map's values. Whether the trigger fires for one opportunity or two hundred, it uses exactly one query and one DML statement. This is the difference between code that works in a demo and code that survives a data-import of thousands of records.</p>
<p>Practical guidance: assume every trigger and every Apex entry point can receive many records. Monitor consumption with <code>Limits.getQueries()</code> and <code>Limits.getDmlStatements()</code> while developing. Cache query results, avoid redundant SOQL, and keep heap under control by querying only the fields you need. If you genuinely must process large volumes asynchronously, that's exactly what Batch Apex is for — which is where we go next.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Asynchronous Apex: Future, Queueable, Batch</h2>
<p>Sometimes work shouldn't — or can't — run in the user's synchronous transaction: long-running callouts, processing thousands of records, or operations that need their own governor limits. <strong>Asynchronous Apex</strong> runs later, on Salesforce's schedule, with a fresh set of higher limits. There are three main flavours, each suited to different jobs.</p>
<p><strong>Future methods</strong> are the simplest: annotate a static <code>void</code> method with <code>@future</code> and it runs asynchronously. They're good for fire-and-forget tasks and for callouts (with <code>@future(callout=true)</code>), but they take only primitive parameters and can't be chained or monitored easily. <strong>Queueable Apex</strong> is the modern upgrade — it's a class, so it can take complex types like sObjects, can be chained by enqueuing another job from within, and returns a job Id you can track.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">// Queueable: takes complex types, can be chained
public class AccountRatingJob implements Queueable {
    private List&lt;Id&gt; accountIds;
    public AccountRatingJob(List&lt;Id&gt; accountIds) {
        this.accountIds = accountIds;
    }
    public void execute(QueueableContext ctx) {
        List&lt;Account&gt; accs = [SELECT Id, AnnualRevenue, Rating
                              FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds];
        for (Account a : accs) {
            a.Rating = (a.AnnualRevenue &gt; 1000000) ? 'Hot' : 'Warm';
        }
        update accs;
    }
}
// Enqueue it
Id jobId = System.enqueueJob(new AccountRatingJob(new List&lt;Id&gt;{ '001...' }));</code></pre>
<p><strong>Batch Apex</strong> is built for large data volumes — up to 50 million records — by splitting the work into chunks. You implement <code>Database.Batchable</code> with three methods: <code>start</code> returns the scope (usually a QueryLocator), <code>execute</code> processes each batch of records (default 200) with fresh limits per batch, and <code>finish</code> runs cleanup or sends a completion email. Because each batch gets its own governor limits, Batch Apex can process millions of records that would never fit in one synchronous transaction. <strong>Scheduled Apex</strong> (<code>Schedulable</code>) can kick off a batch on a cron schedule.</p>
<p>Choosing between them: reach for <strong>Future</strong> only for simple callouts where you don't need tracking, <strong>Queueable</strong> for most async needs because it's flexible and chainable, and <strong>Batch</strong> when the record volume is large enough to need chunking. All async Apex is harder to debug, so test thoroughly with <code>Test.startTest()</code>/<code>Test.stopTest()</code>, which forces async jobs to run synchronously in tests — exactly the subject of our final lesson.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Testing &amp; code coverage</h2>
<p>Salesforce requires at least <strong>75% code coverage</strong> to deploy Apex to production, but coverage is a floor, not a goal. The real purpose of tests is to prove your code behaves correctly and to catch regressions when the org changes. A test that runs your code but asserts nothing hits coverage while verifying nothing — avoid that trap. Good tests set up realistic data, exercise the code, and assert on the outcome.</p>
<p>Apex tests live in classes annotated <code>@isTest</code> and cannot see existing org data by default, so you create the records each test needs. Centralise that creation in a <strong>test data factory</strong> so setup stays DRY. Wrap the code under test in <code>Test.startTest()</code> and <code>Test.stopTest()</code>, which give a fresh set of limits and force asynchronous jobs to complete before assertions run.</p>
<pre><code class="language-apex">@isTest
private class AccountTriggerHandlerTest {

    @TestSetup
    static void makeData() {
        insert new Account(Name = 'Acme', Rating = 'Warm');
    }

    @isTest
    static void ratingBecomesHot_createsFollowUpTask() {
        Account a = [SELECT Id FROM Account LIMIT 1];

        Test.startTest();
        a.Rating = 'Hot';
        update a;
        Test.stopTest();

        List&lt;Task&gt; tasks = [SELECT Id, Subject FROM Task WHERE WhatId = :a.Id];
        System.assertEquals(1, tasks.size(), 'One follow-up task expected');
        System.assertEquals('Follow up: now Hot', tasks[0].Subject);
    }

    @isTest
    static void ratingUnchanged_createsNoTask() {
        Account a = [SELECT Id FROM Account LIMIT 1];
        Test.startTest();
        a.Description = 'edited';
        update a;
        Test.stopTest();
        System.assertEquals(0, [SELECT COUNT() FROM Task WHERE WhatId = :a.Id]);
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>This test class shows the essentials. <code>@TestSetup</code> creates shared data once per class. Each test method targets one behaviour with a descriptive name. Crucially there are <strong>both a positive and a negative case</strong>: one proves the task is created when Rating becomes Hot, the other proves it isn't created otherwise. The assertions check real outcomes with helpful messages, so a failure tells you exactly what broke.</p>
<p>Round out your testing with a few more habits: test <strong>bulk</strong> by inserting 200 records to catch un-bulkified code, test edge cases and error paths, and use <code>System.runAs()</code> to verify behaviour under different user permissions. Never rely on <code>seeAllData=true</code> except where genuinely unavoidable. Aim for tests you'd trust to catch a real bug — coverage above 75% then follows naturally. That completes the masterclass: you can now write Apex that's bulk-safe, limit-aware, and properly tested.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>SAQL basics</h2>
<p>SAQL (Salesforce Analytics Query Language) is the query language that powers every lens and dashboard widget. Most of the time the point-and-click Explorer writes SAQL for you, but learning to read and write it unlocks calculations and shapes the UI can't express, and it's essential for debugging complex dashboards. SAQL is a pipeline language: you load a stream of data from a dataset and pass it through a series of statements, each transforming the stream.</p>
<pre><code class="language-sql">-- Load the dataset into a stream
q = load "Opportunities";

-- Keep only this year's closed-won deals
q = filter q by 'StageName' == "Closed Won" and date('CloseDate_Year','CloseDate_Month','CloseDate_Day') in ["current year"];

-- Group by industry and aggregate
q = group q by 'Account.Industry';
q = foreach q generate
        'Account.Industry' as 'Industry',
        sum('Amount') as 'Total',
        count() as 'Deals';

-- Sort and limit
q = order q by 'Total' desc;
q = limit q 10;</code></pre>
<p>Walk through the pipeline. <code>load</code> pulls a dataset into a stream conventionally named <code>q</code>. <code>filter</code> narrows rows with boolean logic. <code>group</code> declares the dimensions to aggregate by. <code>foreach ... generate</code> is the projection step where you name output fields and apply aggregate functions like <code>sum()</code>, <code>avg()</code>, <code>count()</code>, <code>min()</code>, and <code>max()</code>. <code>order</code> sorts and <code>limit</code> caps the result. The stream flows top to bottom, each line reassigning <code>q</code>.</p>
<p>A few things trip up newcomers. Field names go in single quotes and string literals in double quotes. Dates are decomposed into year/month/day fields in the dataset, so date filtering uses those parts or the <code>date()</code> function as shown. You can run multiple streams and combine them with <code>cogroup</code> to compare measures from different groupings side by side — powerful for things like actual-versus-target.</p>
<p>You don't need to write SAQL for everything, but being able to open a widget's query, understand the pipeline, and tweak a filter or add a computed measure makes you far more capable. Practise by taking a lens you built with clicks, switching to the SAQL editor, and reading how each interaction maps to a line. Next we'll use query results to make widgets talk to each other with bindings.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Bindings &amp; interactions</h2>
<p>Automatic faceting handles the common case where widgets on the same dataset filter each other. <strong>Bindings</strong> take interactivity further: they let one widget's state dynamically change another widget's query, title, columns, or even which measure it shows — including across different datasets. Bindings are what turn a static report into an app that responds to the user.</p>
<p>A binding is an expression, written in the dashboard's JSON, that reads a value from a source (usually a selection or a result) and injects it into a target query. There are two evaluation types. <strong>Selection bindings</strong> react to what a user picks in a widget and are evaluated as the user interacts. <strong>Results bindings</strong> react to the data a query returns, useful for driving one widget from another's computed output. Each uses interaction functions like <code>selection()</code> and <code>results()</code> plus serialization functions such as <code>asString()</code>, <code>asObject()</code>, and <code>coalesce()</code> to format the value for its destination.</p>
<pre><code class="language-json">{
  "start": "{{ column(selection(list_stage), [\"value\"]).asObject() }}",
  "query": {
    "filters": [
      ["StageName", "{{ column(selection(list_stage), [\"value\"]).asObject() }}", "in"]
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Conceptually the example above reads the selected values from a stage list widget (<code>list_stage</code>) and injects them into another widget's filter, so choosing stages in one place reshapes another chart that couldn't otherwise be faceted automatically. The double-brace <code>{{ }}</code> delimits the binding expression; <code>selection()</code> grabs the user's pick, <code>column()</code> extracts the relevant column, and <code>asObject()</code> serialises it into the array form the filter expects.</p>
<p>Bindings are the most technical part of dashboard building, so a few tips help. Start from faceting and only reach for bindings when you need cross-dataset interactivity or dynamic titles, columns, and measures. Give widgets clear step names because you reference them by name in expressions. Build and test incrementally, checking the preview after each binding. Provide sensible defaults with <code>coalesce()</code> so the dashboard behaves before the user selects anything. Master bindings and you can build genuinely dynamic analytics apps — the capstone skill of this course.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Component anatomy: HTML, JS, meta</h2>
<p>Every Lightning Web Component is a folder containing three files that share the component's name. If your component is <code>helloWorld</code>, the folder holds <code>helloWorld.html</code> (the template), <code>helloWorld.js</code> (the JavaScript class), and <code>helloWorld.js-meta.xml</code> (the configuration). Understanding what each does — and how they connect — is the foundation for everything else.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">// helloWorld.js
import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';

export default class HelloWorld extends LightningElement {
    greeting = 'World';

    handleChange(event) {
        this.greeting = event.target.value;
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>The <strong>JavaScript file</strong> defines a class that extends <code>LightningElement</code>, the base class every component inherits from. Public class fields become the component's state and are automatically reactive for primitives. Methods handle events and logic. The class is the default export, and its name is conventionally the PascalCase version of the folder name.</p>
<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- helloWorld.html --&gt;
&lt;template&gt;
    &lt;lightning-input label="Name" value={greeting}
                     onchange={handleChange}&gt;&lt;/lightning-input&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Hello, {greeting}!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The <strong>HTML file</strong> is always wrapped in a single root <code>&lt;template&gt;</code> tag. Inside, curly braces bind to properties and getters on the class (<code>{greeting}</code>), and <code>on</code>-prefixed attributes wire DOM events to handler methods (<code>onchange={handleChange}</code>). You compose base components like <code>lightning-input</code> from the Salesforce component library rather than reinventing form controls.</p>
<p>The <strong>meta XML file</strong> configures where the component can be used. It sets the API version, whether the component is exposed (<code>isExposed</code>), and which <strong>targets</strong> it supports — App Page, Record Page, Home Page, Flow, and more — along with any design properties admins can set. Without exposing it and declaring targets, the component won't appear in the Lightning App Builder. With these three files understood, we can dig into how state changes drive the UI, which is reactivity — the next lesson.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Reactivity, getters &amp; template directives</h2>
<p>Reactivity is what makes a component's UI update automatically when its data changes. In LWC, any field on the class is reactive for primitive values — assign a new string or number and the template re-renders. For objects and arrays, reactivity is deeper only when you reassign the reference or use the <code>@track</code> decorator, so mutating a nested property in place may not re-render unless you replace the object. The practical rule: assign new values rather than mutating in place, and reach for <code>@track</code> when you need deep observation of an object or array.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { LightningElement, track } from 'lwc';

export default class CartSummary extends LightningElement {
    items = [
        { id: '1', name: 'Platform Licence', price: 50 },
        { id: '2', name: 'Analytics Add-on', price: 30 }
    ];

    // A getter derives value from state and is reactive
    get total() {
        return this.items.reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.price, 0);
    }
    get hasItems() {
        return this.items.length &gt; 0;
    }
}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Getters</strong> are the idiomatic way to compute derived values. Rather than storing a total you must keep in sync, expose a getter that recomputes from source state; the template calls it like a property (<code>{total}</code>) and it re-evaluates whenever its inputs change. Getters keep templates clean and logic testable, and they're preferred over doing computation in the markup, which LWC doesn't allow anyway — you can't call functions with arguments in the template.</p>
<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;template&gt;
    &lt;template lwc:if={hasItems}&gt;
        &lt;ul&gt;
            &lt;template for:each={items} for:item="line"&gt;
                &lt;li key={line.id}&gt;{line.name} — {line.price}&lt;/li&gt;
            &lt;/template&gt;
        &lt;/ul&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Total: {total}&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/template&gt;
    &lt;template lwc:else&gt;
        &lt;p&gt;Your cart is empty.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/template&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>
<p>The template <strong>directives</strong> control rendering. <code>lwc:if</code>, <code>lwc:elseif</code>, and <code>lwc:else</code> conditionally render blocks (replacing the older <code>if:true</code>/<code>if:false</code>). <code>for:each</code> with <code>for:item</code> iterates a list, and every iterated element needs a unique <code>key</code> so LWC can efficiently track changes — the key must be a stable id, never the index. There's also <code>iterator:</code> when you need first/last flags. Master these and you can render any data-driven UI. Next we make components interactive by handling and dispatching events.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Handling events &amp; component communication</h2>
<p>Components rarely live alone, so communication between them is essential. LWC follows the web-standard event model, and the guiding principle is <strong>data down, events up</strong>: parents pass data to children through public properties, and children notify parents by dispatching events. Getting this direction right keeps components reusable and reasoning simple.</p>
<p>A <strong>parent passes data down</strong> by exposing a public property on the child with the <code>@api</code> decorator, then binding to it in markup. A <strong>child sends data up</strong> by creating and dispatching a <code>CustomEvent</code>, optionally carrying a payload in its <code>detail</code>. The parent listens with an <code>on</code>-prefixed handler.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Child: tile.js
import { LightningElement, api } from 'lwc';

export default class Tile extends LightningElement {
    @api product; // set by the parent

    handleSelect() {
        // Notify the parent, passing data in detail
        this.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('select', {
            detail: { productId: this.product.id }
        }));
    }
}</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Parent template --&gt;
&lt;template for:each={products} for:item="p"&gt;
    &lt;c-tile key={p.id} product={p} onselect={handleSelect}&gt;&lt;/c-tile&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>
<p>In the parent's <code>handleSelect(event)</code> you read <code>event.detail.productId</code> to know which tile fired. A few rules keep events clean: event names are lowercase with no spaces, dispatched via <code>this.dispatchEvent()</code>, and by default they don't cross the shadow boundary unless you set <code>bubbles</code> and <code>composed</code> — which you should use sparingly because it couples components. Prefer targeted parent-child events over broadly bubbling ones.</p>
<p>Not all components are in a parent-child relationship, though. For <strong>sibling or unrelated components</strong>, use the <strong>Lightning Message Service (LMS)</strong>, which publishes and subscribes to messages over a message channel across the DOM — even between LWC, Aura, and Visualforce. For deep component trees you can also use the <strong>pubsub</strong> pattern, but LMS is the supported, modern choice. Choose the lightest mechanism that fits: <code>@api</code> down and <code>CustomEvent</code> up within a hierarchy, LMS across the page. With components talking to each other, the next step is bringing in real Salesforce data.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Wiring Apex &amp; Lightning Data Service</h2>
<p>A UI is only useful with real data, and LWC offers two main ways to read and write Salesforce records: <strong>Lightning Data Service (LDS)</strong> and <strong>Apex</strong>. LDS is the preferred default for single-record operations because it's declarative, cache-aware, and keeps records in sync across components without you writing server code. Apex is for anything LDS can't express — complex queries, multi-object logic, and custom server processing.</p>
<p>The <strong>wire service</strong> is how components reactively consume data. You decorate a property or function with <code>@wire</code>, point it at an adapter, and LWC calls it, caches the result, and re-invokes it when reactive parameters change. LDS provides wire adapters like <code>getRecord</code> and <code>getObjectInfo</code> from the <code>lightning/ui*Api</code> modules, while the base components <code>lightning-record-form</code>, <code>lightning-record-edit-form</code>, and <code>lightning-record-view-form</code> give you create/edit/view UI with almost no code.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { LightningElement, api, wire } from 'lwc';
import { getRecord, getFieldValue } from 'lightning/uiRecordApi';
import NAME_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Name';
import INDUSTRY_FIELD from '@salesforce/schema/Account.Industry';

// Apex, wired reactively
import getContacts from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.getContacts';

export default class AccountPanel extends LightningElement {
    @api recordId;

    @wire(getRecord, { recordId: '$recordId', fields: [NAME_FIELD, INDUSTRY_FIELD] })
    account;

    @wire(getContacts, { accountId: '$recordId' })
    contacts;

    get accountName() {
        return getFieldValue(this.account.data, NAME_FIELD);
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Note the <code>'$recordId'</code> syntax: prefixing a parameter with <code>$</code> makes it <strong>reactive</strong>, so when <code>recordId</code> changes the wire re-runs automatically. Importing fields from <code>@salesforce/schema</code> gives compile-time safety and makes Salesforce aware of the dependency. To call <strong>Apex</strong>, the method must be <code>@AuraEnabled</code>; mark it <code>(cacheable=true)</code> to use it with <code>@wire</code> for read-only, cacheable data, or call it imperatively (returning a Promise) when you need to invoke it on demand, such as on a button click, or when writing data.</p>
<p>Choosing between them: use <strong>LDS</strong> for single-record read/create/update/delete because it caches and shares records automatically; use <strong>cacheable Apex with @wire</strong> for read-only lists and queries; use <strong>imperative Apex</strong> for writes and actions triggered by user interaction. Always handle both the <code>data</code> and <code>error</code> branches a wire provides. With data flowing, the next lesson adds the platform niceties users expect — navigation, toasts, and error handling.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Navigation, toasts &amp; errors</h2>
<p>Beyond rendering data, real components need to move users around, give feedback, and fail gracefully. Salesforce provides platform modules for each of these so your components behave consistently with the rest of Lightning Experience. The three you'll reach for constantly are <strong>NavigationMixin</strong> for navigation, <strong>ShowToastEvent</strong> for notifications, and a solid pattern for surfacing errors.</p>
<p><strong>Navigation</strong> uses the <code>NavigationMixin</code> applied to your component's class. You build a <em>page reference</em> — a descriptor of where to go, such as a record page, a list view, a web page, or a component — and pass it to <code>this[NavigationMixin.Navigate](pageRef)</code>. This keeps navigation declarative and portable across desktop and mobile.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { LightningElement } from 'lwc';
import { NavigationMixin } from 'lightning/navigation';
import { ShowToastEvent } from 'lightning/platformShowToastEvent';
import saveRecord from '@salesforce/apex/AccountController.save';

export default class AccountActions extends NavigationMixin(LightningElement) {
    async handleSave() {
        try {
            const recordId = await saveRecord({ /* ... */ });
            this.showToast('Success', 'Account saved', 'success');
            this[NavigationMixin.Navigate]({
                type: 'standard__recordPage',
                attributes: { recordId, objectApiName: 'Account', actionName: 'view' }
            });
        } catch (error) {
            this.showToast('Error', this.reduceError(error), 'error');
        }
    }

    showToast(title, message, variant) {
        this.dispatchEvent(new ShowToastEvent({ title, message, variant }));
    }

    reduceError(error) {
        return (error?.body?.message) || error?.message || 'Unknown error';
    }
}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Toasts</strong> are dispatched by firing a <code>ShowToastEvent</code> with a title, message, and <code>variant</code> (<code>success</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>warning</code>, or <code>info</code>). They're the standard way to confirm an action or report a problem without a modal. Keep messages short and specific.</p>
<p><strong>Error handling</strong> deserves care because Apex and wire errors arrive in different shapes. A wire error lands in the adapter's <code>error</code> property; an imperative call rejects a Promise you catch. The messages nest differently — sometimes in <code>error.body.message</code>, sometimes in an array of page errors — so a small reducer like <code>reduceError</code> that normalises them into a readable string is a common, worthwhile utility. Always show the user something meaningful rather than a silent failure. With interactive, resilient components built, it's time to prove they work — testing with Jest is next.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}

{"root":{"children":[{"type":"html","version":1,"html":"<h2>Testing with Jest</h2>
<p>Lightning Web Components are tested with <strong>Jest</strong>, the popular JavaScript testing framework, via the <code>@salesforce/sfdx-lwc-jest</code> wrapper that knows how to render LWC. Unit tests run locally in Node — fast, no org required — and verify that a component renders correctly, reacts to input, dispatches the right events, and handles data and errors. Good Jest coverage catches regressions long before they reach an org.</p>
<p>Tests live in a <code>__tests__</code> folder inside the component and follow a consistent rhythm: create the element, append it to the document to render it, then query its shadow DOM and assert. Because rendering is asynchronous, you await a microtask (commonly <code>return Promise.resolve()</code>) before checking the DOM.</p>
<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { createElement } from 'lwc';
import CartSummary from 'c/cartSummary';

describe('c-cart-summary', () =&gt; {
    afterEach(() =&gt; {
        while (document.body.firstChild) {
            document.body.removeChild(document.body.firstChild);
        }
    });

    it('renders the total from its items', () =&gt; {
        const element = createElement('c-cart-summary', { is: CartSummary });
        document.body.appendChild(element);

        return Promise.resolve().then(() =&gt; {
            const total = element.shadowRoot.querySelector('p.total');
            expect(total.textContent).toBe('Total: 80');
        });
    });

    it('fires a select event when a tile is clicked', () =&gt; {
        const element = createElement('c-cart-summary', { is: CartSummary });
        const handler = jest.fn();
        element.addEventListener('select', handler);
        document.body.appendChild(element);

        return Promise.resolve().then(() =&gt; {
            element.shadowRoot.querySelector('button').click();
            expect(handler).toHaveBeenCalled();
        });
    });
});</code></pre>
<p>The pattern is consistent: <code>createElement</code> builds the component, <code>appendChild</code> renders it, and <code>shadowRoot.querySelector</code> reaches into its DOM. You simulate interaction by calling <code>click()</code> or dispatching events, and assert with Jest matchers. To test events, attach a <code>jest.fn()</code> listener and assert it was called with the expected <code>detail</code>. Always clean up in <code>afterEach</code> so tests don't leak DOM between cases.</p>
<p>Two things make LWC tests robust. First, <strong>mock Apex</strong> by pointing Jest at a mock module for <code>@salesforce/apex/...</code> imports so tests don't need a server. Second, <strong>emit wire data</strong> using the wire adapters' test utilities (the <code>emit</code> method on a test adapter) to feed both success and error data into <code>@wire</code> properties, letting you assert how the component renders each branch. Test the states users actually hit — loading, data, empty, and error. With components tested, the final step is getting them into an org, which we cover next.</p>"}],"direction":"ltr","format":"","indent":0,"type":"root","version":1}}
